    <statement><p>Find the gradient of the function
<m>f(x,y,z) = yz^{2}</m>, at the point <m>(0,-1,1)</m></p>
<p><m>\nabla f(0,-1,1) =</m> <fillin characters="55" name="AnSwEr0001"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\,\mathit{\vec j}-2\,\mathit{\vec k}</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><m>f_x=0</m>,  <m>f_y=z^{2}</m> and  <m>f_z =y\cdot 2z</m>.
Thus <m>\mbox{grad} f = \boldsymbol{0}+z^{2}\,\mathit{\vec j}+y\cdot 2z\,\mathit{\vec k}</m> and
<m>\nabla f(0,-1,1) = \,\mathit{\vec j}-2\,\mathit{\vec k}</m>.




</solution>
